Development tests and tools for scubed3
=======================================

This directory contains the tests and the tools that were used while
developing scubed3: torture tests for growing, shrinking, crashes and torn
writes, tests with real filesystems on top, statistical tests of the write
order (paranoia levels), and offline tools that read a base file without
the daemon.

They are not needed to use scubed3.


Requirements
~~~~~~~~~~~~

- Linux with FUSE (libfuse 3; the tests unmount with fusermount3, or with
  fusermount if that is not there), run as root (FUSE mounts, loop devices,
  mount(8))
- python3, with the "cryptography" module for s3read.py and s3diff.py (and
  the tests that use s3read.py: corrupttest.py, ioerrtest.py, fsrepair.py)
- e2fsprogs (mkfs.ext2/3/4, e2fsck, resize2fs), util-linux (losetup, fstrim,
  mount, runuser, script), coreutils (timeout)
- for sockettest.sh: the user "nobody"
- optional: xfsprogs, see discardtest.py
- the mountpoint /mnt/s3 (torture.py creates it, the other tests expect it),
  the tests create their own mountpoints /mnt/... for the filesystems
- NO OTHER scubed3 daemon may be running: the tests use the default control
  socket /run/scubed3/control and the mountpoint /mnt/s3

The tests create their base files (and logs) in the current directory, or in
$S3BUILD if it is set; most of them are 64 MiB to 1.6 GiB.


Building
~~~~~~~~

	sh dev/build.sh [OUTDIR]

builds, from ../src, in OUTDIR (default: the current directory):

  scubed3        the daemon
  scubed3-crash  the daemon with -DSCUBED3_CRASH_TEST, it has test hooks
                 (see below), several tests need it
  scubed3ctl     the control program
  jstat          the unit test of juggler.c (jstat.c)

configure must have been run in the main tree (config.h). EXTRA_CFLAGS and
EXTRA_LDFLAGS are added to the compiler and linker flags (for example for
libraries in a non-standard place). The tests take the NAME of the daemon
binary, they look for it in the current directory (or $S3BUILD).

Test hooks of scubed3-crash (environment variables of the daemon):

  SCUBED3_CRASH_AFTER=N  exit (as if the power fails) before write N+1 of a
                         macroblock
  SCUBED3_TEAR=1         with SCUBED3_CRASH_AFTER: a random subset of the
                         4 KiB pieces of that write reaches the disk (torn
                         write)
  SCUBED3_BAD_READ=list  reads that include one of the byte offsets in list
                         fail with EIO (a bad sector), until it is written
  SCUBED3_WRITE_LOG=file the offset of every macroblock write is appended
                         to file (used for the statistical tests)
  SCUBED3_WRITE_LOG_TIME=1  with SCUBED3_WRITE_LOG: the time of the write
                         (CLOCK_MONOTONIC) is logged too
  SCUBED3_IV_LOG=file    every block write of a partition is appended to
                         file before the write: partition, block, seqno,
                         nonce (used by ivtest.py)


Running everything
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

	mkdir /mnt/s3; cd OUTDIR; sh /path/to/dev/batch.sh

runs all tests below, one after the other (this takes a few hours); every
test writes NAME.out, the last line of every test is collected in
batch.summary (a test that passes ends with "... OK"; the torture runs end
with "FINAL OK"). MKFS_XFS=path/to/mkfs.xfs adds XFS to discardtest.py.


Torture tests
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

torture.py DAEMON STEPS SEED [crash|tear]
	The main test: random steps on a small store (40 macroblocks of
	4 MiB): writes, fsync, discards (punched holes and zeroed ranges), grow,
	shrink (also to the minimum of 2 blocks), restarts, and (with crash or
	tear, needs scubed3-crash) crashes and torn writes at random moments,
	also during a resize and while removals are pending. An oracle knows
	what every mesoblock may contain: after a clean restart exactly what was
	written last, after a crash what was written up to the last fsync or
	something newer, discarded data never comes back, a grown part reads
	as zeroes. At the end it checks that the tombstones of the discards die
	out (not in level 0, see the main README). PARANOIA=0, 2 or "3 KIBPS"
	runs it in that paranoia level (in level 2 and 3 the free space is
	overwritten too; in level 3 the daemon also writes while nothing is
	written, so it can crash at any moment). CRASH_MAX (default 12) is the
	maximum number of writes before a crash.
	Ends with "FINAL OK: ..." and statistics of what happened.

runall.sh, runall0.sh, runall2.sh, runall3.sh
	8 torture runs in level 1 (normal, crash and tear), 5 in level 0, 5 in
	level 2 and 5 shorter ones in level 3 (with a rate of 400 MiB/s: as
	fast as the disk can), a summary in runall.out, runall0.out,
	runall2.out, runall3.out.


Filesystems on top
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

fstest.py DAEMON CYCLES SEED
	ext2 through grow/shrink cycles: every cycle shrinks the filesystem
	(resize2fs) and the partition, restarts the daemon while the shrink is
	not finished, writes files, grows the partition and the filesystem;
	after every step e2fsck -fn and the checksums of all files.

killtest.py DAEMON ROUNDS SEED
	ext4: write files, sync, write more, kill -9 the daemon; after the
	restart the files written before the sync must be intact and e2fsck
	must find the filesystem clean. DISCARD=1 mounts with -o discard and
	runs fstrim before the kill.

discardtest.py DAEMON SEED
	Discard (fallocate PUNCH_HOLE and ZERO_RANGE, fstrim): a byte exact
	model with unaligned punch/zero/write and restarts; completely full
	partitions; tombstones die out; blocks that come back with a grow with
	old indices of the partition on them; ext2 (with the ext4 driver),
	ext3, ext4 with -o discard, and XFS if MKFS_XFS is set (xfs_repair is
	taken from $XFS_REPAIR, from the xfsprogs build tree of mkfs.xfs, or
	from $PATH): fstrim frees mesoblocks, nothing changes at a restart,
	fsck is clean, the write cost with and without fstrim.
	PARTS=ABCD selects parts of the test.

sizetest.py DAEMON
	Non-default mesoblock and macroblock sizes (-m 12..16, -M 20..24): ext4
	with files, fstrim, restart, e2fsck, contents.

fsrepair.py
	ext4 on top (uses scubed3 from the current directory): corrupt a 4 KiB
	block of a file on disk, reading the file gives EIO, rewriting the file
	in place repairs it, e2fsck stays clean.


Integrity, reads and writes, openers
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

corrupttest.py DAEMON ROUNDS SEED
	Flips bits in mesoblocks on disk: reading the corrupt 4 KiB parts gives
	EIO (and only those), check-data-integrity reports exactly them, also
	after garbage collection and restarts; a partial write into a corrupt
	part fails, writing a whole part repairs it.

ioerrtest.py DAEMON-crash
	The return values of reads and writes: at the end of the partition
	(short reads and writes, 0 at the end, ENOSPC for writes at the end),
	also with -o direct_io; read errors of the base device
	(SCUBED3_BAD_READ): the daemon keeps running, the mesoblock gives EIO,
	check-data-integrity reports it, rewriting repairs it; an unreadable
	index of an unallocated block is harmless, of a block of the partition:
	the partition is not opened (the daemon keeps running).

openertest.py DAEMON SCUBED3CTL ROUNDS
	Several openers of a partition and no EBUSY races: two file descriptors
	at once, mkfs/resize2fs/e2fsck directly on the partition, close and
	resize right after umount or close() without retries, close of a
	partition that is really in use, close-on-release with two openers,
	scubed3ctl refuses to mount twice.

truncatetest.py DAEMON
	Truncate behaves as on a block device: dd without conv=notrunc,
	"> partition", truncate(1) and ftruncate() succeed, but neither the size
	nor the contents change.


scubed3ctl and the control socket
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

configtest.sh DAEMON SCUBED3CTL
	KDF parameters from the configuration file: a partition created with
	the salt from the file is found with the key PBKDF2-SHA256(passphrase,
	salt, iterations), -C and /etc/scubed3.conf, -s/-i override the file,
	errors in the file are fatal, also an invalid paranoia= (uses
	script(1) to type the passphrase).

opentime.py DAEMON [BLOCKS]
	How long it takes to open a partition on a large base device (BLOCKS
	macroblocks of 32 KiB, default 29000, three small partitions): with
	the page cache dropped, every partition is opened twice, and a
	passphrase without a partition; the first open reads the start of
	the index of every block, the later ones only decrypt it (scubed3
	keeps it in memory); the partitions must contain what was written.

rootswitchtest.py DAEMON SEED
	scubed3 keeps working when its root directory is emptied after its
	start (root on scubed3: run-init deletes the files of the initramfs):
	the daemon is started in a chroot with its libraries, /dev/fuse,
	/dev/urandom and the base file, then everything in the chroot is
	deleted; partitions are created, written, closed, opened again and
	read, also in paranoia level 3; afterwards a normal daemon finds the
	data in the base file (a hard link outside the chroot).

initramfstest.sh DAEMON SCUBED3CTL
	The local-top script of contrib/initramfs-tools in a simulated
	initramfs (its paths point into a temporary directory, a stand-in for
	/scripts/functions, the answers typed with script(1)): a wrong
	passphrase is asked again, another partition is opened, Yes for level
	2; afterwards ROOT in param.conf is a loop device with the file system
	of the root partition, the daemon runs as @scubed3 (with oom_score_adj
	-1000, where that can be set), both partitions are open, level 2.
	Uses /dev/scubed3fs and /run/scubed3/control (like a boot), no other
	daemon may run.

lukstest.sh DAEMON SCUBED3CTL
	LUKS on top of a partition with scubed3ctl, and the warning below the
	recommended paranoia level: in level 1 mount and luksformat warn, an
	answer other than yes cancels; in level 2 luksformat, mke2fs and mount
	work on the LUKS volume, umount closes it, a file survives umount and
	mount, its plaintext is not in the partition. Needs cryptsetup and the
	device mapper (it says SKIPPED without cryptsetup); with
	LUKSTEST_FAKE=1 the cryptsetup in PATH may be a stand-in without
	encryption (the plaintext check is skipped).

sockettest.sh DAEMON SCUBED3CTL
	The control socket: private directory (0700) and socket (0600), unsafe
	directories refused, no takeover of a running daemon, takeover of a
	stale socket, the peer check (as user nobody), the socket is removed at
	exit.


Paranoia levels and the write order
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

ivtest.py DAEMON-crash SEED [OLD-DAEMON]
	The IVs of the data mesoblocks (ESSIV(seqno^nonce, slot, block)) do
	not repeat when the seqnos repeat: a backup of the base device is
	restored and used again (after every fsync the indices are read with
	the key), and interrupted writes (kill -9, torn) are redone (the IV of
	every block write is logged, SCUBED3_IV_LOG: the interrupted write is
	not seen in the indices, its data is under the old index); the test
	fails if one (block, seqno^nonce) belongs to two different writes (it
	also checks that the seqnos did repeat); with OLD-DAEMON (a scubed3 of version 0.4) a base device
	written by it is opened, read and written, both versions of blocks
	are read.

wipetest.py DAEMON-crash SEED
	Removing a partition wipes every block that has an index of it: in
	level 1 at once (also the blocks it gave away in an earlier shrink); in
	level 3 in random order, while it cannot be closed or resized and its
	key cannot be used; kill -9 (also with a torn write) while it is
	removed or wiped never brings its data back, opening it again resumes
	the removal or the wipe (in level 1 at once, in level 3 in random
	order) and afterwards no block has an index of it; a partition whose
	old index uses blocks of another open partition is not opened (and the
	daemon keeps running); a partition of 2 blocks (the minimum) is
	removed in level 3.

waitreadtest.py DAEMON SECONDS SEED LEVEL [KIBPS] [MAXREAD]
	A writer that waits for its turn (paranoia level 2 or 3; level 3 with
	KIBPS, default 640 KiB/s: 10 writes per second) does not block the
	reads of its partition: one thread writes partition a as fast as
	possible, other threads read a and b and measure how long a read
	takes (median, 99%, max; with MAXREAD in seconds the test fails if a
	read of a takes longer); every mesoblock contains its index and a
	version, a read must return a complete version that is not older than
	the last one written; at the end and after a restart a and b contain
	exactly what was written.

churntest.py DAEMON ROUNDS SEED LEVEL [KIBPS]
	Partitions are created, enlarged, shrunk to 0 blocks (removed) and
	closed, over and over, while two other partitions are written as fast
	as possible, in paranoia level LEVEL (0 to 3; level 3 with KIBPS,
	default 6400 KiB/s: 100 writes per second); the new partition must
	contain what was written to it, the level must stay the same, the block
	counts must add up, the other partitions must contain exactly what
	was written to them (also after a restart), and in the end no block may
	have an index of a removed partition (in level 2 and 3 the test waits
	until it is wiped before it closes it).

paranoiatest.py DAEMON-crash SCUBED3CTL SEED
	Level 2 with three partitions and free space (small blocks: -m 12
	-M 16): the written blocks are uniform over the whole base device and
	consecutive writes independent (chi-square, writing to one partition
	only), level 1 for comparison; data correct with writes, discards,
	fsync, restarts and kill -9; three writers and a control prober at the
	same time (no deadlock); scubed3ctl paranoia 2; closing a partition
	switches back to level 1; a partition that is not open is overwritten
	(as documented).

resizestat.py DAEMON-crash ROUNDS SEED
	Level 2 while a partition is grown and shrunk again many times: the
	blocks added by a grow are hit as often as any other block in the
	writes right after the grow, the oldest blocks of the partition (most
	of them are removed) as often as any other block during the shrink,
	all writes uniform and independent. Only things an observer could know
	beforehand are used to choose the blocks that are counted, and the
	first writes of a shrink are skipped (the comment in the script
	explains why: the time between writes depends on the live data of the
	next block). Every z must be small (|z| < 4); 1500 rounds take about
	ten minutes.

memoryless.py DAEMON-crash WRITES [FSYNC-PROBABILITY]
	Level 1, one partition, no resizes: the block that is written does not
	depend on how long ago it was written (age quartiles uniform, chi2 with
	3 degrees of freedom).

level0test.py DAEMON-crash SEED
	Level 0: fewer block writes than level 1 for the same data, the next
	block is the block with the least live data, data correct after a
	restart.

level3test.py DAEMON-crash SCUBED3CTL SEED
	Level 3 (10 writes per second, 64 KiB blocks): idle, the writes come at
	the rate and the times between them are exponentially distributed;
	while a partition is written as fast as possible, the same rate and
	distribution and the written blocks uniform over the base device, the
	throughput of the partition at most its share of the rate; data correct
	with fsync, a clean restart and kill -9; the paranoia command answers
	at once while a writer waits for its turn, and switching to level 1
	lets the writer finish; scubed3ctl paranoia 3 KIBPS; closing a
	partition switches back to level 1 and the writes stop.

jstat STEPS (jstat.c, built by build.sh)
	Unit test of juggler.c, without the daemon: every draw is uniform over
	the pool at that moment, also while blocks are added and removed
	(chi-square), self-draws and run lengths as expected for i.i.d. draws.

drawstat.py LOG...
	The rate at which a block is drawn as its own next block in daemon logs
	(the daemon logs every draw), compared with the i.i.d. expectation.


Offline tools
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

s3read.py [-s SALT] BASE PASSPHRASE KDF_ITERATIONS [OUT_IMAGE]
	An independent reader of the on-disk format (SSS3v0.5 and v0.4,
	written from the description in the main README, it shares no code
	with scubed3): lists
	the macroblocks of the partition of the passphrase, ignores a torn most
	recent block, and with OUT_IMAGE writes the reconstructed partition
	(corrupt 4 KiB parts as zeroes, they are reported). Some tests use it to
	find mesoblocks on disk. Default salt "scubed3_prod", PBKDF2-SHA256,
	4 MiB macroblocks, 16 KiB mesoblocks.

s3diff.py [-s SALT] BEFORE AFTER PASSPHRASE KDF_ITERATIONS
	The view of an adversary who has ONE passphrase and two snapshots of
	the base file: which macroblocks changed, and do they all belong to the
	partition of that passphrase? In paranoia level 1 a changed block that
	is not ours shows that another partition was written; in level 2 the
	free space is overwritten too, so it shows nothing.
